  "Number": 26,
  "Question": "If \\(\\log_{10}m=b-\\log_{10}n,\\) then \\(m=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\dfrac{b}{n}\\)",
    "B": "\\(bn\\)",
    "C": "\\(10^bn\\)",
    "D": "\\(b-10^n\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{10^b}{n}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The real logarithms require \\(m>0\\) and \\(n>0\\). Add \\(\\log_{10}n\\) to both sides so that the product rule can combine the two logarithms:\n\\[\n\\log_{10}(mn)=\\log_{10}m+\\log_{10}n=b.\n\\]\nBy the definition of a base-10 logarithm, this means \\(mn=10^b\\). Because \\(n>0\\), division by \\(n\\) is valid, giving\n\\[\nm=\\frac{10^b}{n}.\n\\]\nThis is choice \\(\\mathrm{E}\\).",
